### Step 2: Update only the `bellTooltip` getter

Find the existing `bellTooltip` getter near the `normalizeValue()` method. Replace only that getter with this version:

```typescript

get bellTooltip(): string {
  if (!this.enableBellIcon()) {
    return this.ALERTS_UNAVAILABLE_TOOLTIP;
  }

  if (this.notificationResource?.length) {
    const recipients = this.notificationResource
      .map(item =>
        this.phoneFormat.transform(item['communicationValue'])
      )
      .join('\n');

    return `(${this.notificationResource.length}) Alert Recipients\n${recipients}`;
  }

  return ACTIONS_SIDEBAR_MENU.ALERT_NOTIFY;
}
```

This preserves the existing tooltip behavior for eligible shipments and adds the required message for ineligible ones.

### Step 3: Replace only the bell button in the HTML

In `actions-sidebar-cell-renderer.component.html`, find the first `@if` block containing the bell icon.

Replace that block with the following:

```html

@if (this.pageName === HEADER_CONSTANTS.TRACKING.route) {
  <span
    [matTooltip]="bellTooltip"
    matTooltipClass="multiline-tooltip"
    matTooltipPosition="above"
    style="display: inline-flex"
  >
    <opti-button
      [centerIcon]="
        !enableBellIcon()
          ? 'notifications_off'
          : (icontoggle ? 'notifications_active' : 'notifications_none')
      "
      buttonType="secondary"
      [disabled]="!enableBellIcon()"
      (click)="onAlert()"
    ></opti-button>
  </span>
}
```

Why this works:

* `notifications_off` displays the slashed bell when alerts are unavailable.

* `[disabled]="!enableBellIcon()"` disables the button in that state.

* The tooltip is attached to the wrapper so it can still appear when the button is disabled.

* When alerts are available, your existing subscribed and unsubscribed icons remain unchanged.

Keep the rest of the HTML exactly as it is, including the new Shipment Protection Claim menu item.

